  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q33-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p10-q33-conjecture-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Goldbach’s conjecture",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is one of the oldest and best-known unsolved problems in number theory. It states that every even natural number greater than 2 is the sum of two prime numbers.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q33-a-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that every even number from 4 to 20 is the sum of two prime numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\begin{aligned}4&=2+2\\\\6&=3+3\\\\8&=3+5\\\\10&=5+5\\\\12&=5+7\\\\14&=7+7\\\\16&=3+13\\\\18&=5+13\\\\20&=3+17\\end{aligned}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4&=2+2\\\\6&=3+3\\\\8&=3+5\\\\10&=5+5\\\\12&=5+7\\\\14&=7+7\\\\16&=3+13\\\\18&=5+13\\\\20&=3+17\\end{align*}$$\n\nEvery summand listed is prime.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q33-odd-group",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p10-q33-odd-context-paragraph",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Some odd numbers are also the sum of two primes.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p10-q33-b-node",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "b",
            "prompt": "Show that 99 is the sum of two primes.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$99=2+97$.",
              "worked": "$$99=2+97.$$\n\nBoth 2 and 97 are prime: 97 has no prime divisor among 2, 3, 5 and 7, the primes less than $\\sqrt{97}$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 28
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q33-c-node",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "c",
            "prompt": "Show that 101 is not the sum of two primes.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": {
                "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
                "version": 1,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "id": "p10-q33-c-short-result",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "fontSize": null,
                    "align": "left",
                    "spaceBefore": 0,
                    "spaceAfter": 2,
                    "lineHeight": 1.4,
                    "indent": 0,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "101 is not the sum of two primes.",
                        "marks": []
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "p10-q33-c-short-reason",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "fontSize": null,
                    "align": "left",
                    "spaceBefore": 0,
                    "spaceAfter": 2,
                    "lineHeight": 1.4,
                    "indent": 0,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "An odd sum requires one prime to be 2, but ",
                        "marks": []
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "101-2=99=3\\times33",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " is composite.",
                        "marks": []
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              "worked": "Suppose 101 is the sum of two primes. Two odd primes have an even sum, so one prime must be 2. The other would be\n$$\\begin{align*}101-2&=99\\\\101-2&=3\\times33.\\end{align*}$$\nSince 99 is composite, this is impossible. Therefore, 101 is not the sum of two primes.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 52
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q33-d-node",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "d",
            "prompt": "Describe the odd numbers that are the sum of two primes.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": {
                "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
                "version": 1,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "id": "p10-q33-d-short-result",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "fontSize": null,
                    "align": "left",
                    "spaceBefore": 0,
                    "spaceAfter": 2,
                    "lineHeight": 1.4,
                    "indent": 0,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Precisely the odd numbers ",
                        "marks": []
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "n",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " for which ",
                        "marks": []
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "n-2",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " is prime.",
                        "marks": []
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "p10-q33-d-short-reason",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "fontSize": null,
                    "align": "left",
                    "spaceBefore": 0,
                    "spaceAfter": 2,
                    "lineHeight": 1.4,
                    "indent": 0,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "These are 2 plus an odd prime.",
                        "marks": []
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              "worked": "If an odd number $n$ is the sum of two primes, one summand must be even. The only even prime is 2, so $n-2$ must be prime.\n\nConversely, if $n$ is odd and $n-2$ is prime, then $n=2+(n-2)$ is a sum of two primes. Thus the required numbers are exactly 2 plus an odd prime.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 36
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q33-e-node",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "e",
            "prompt": "If we assume Goldbach’s conjecture is true, then show that every odd number greater than 5 is the sum of three primes.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": {
                "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
                "version": 1,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "id": "p10-q33-e-short-result",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "fontSize": null,
                    "align": "left",
                    "spaceBefore": 0,
                    "spaceAfter": 2,
                    "lineHeight": 1.4,
                    "indent": 0,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "n=3+p+q",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": ", where ",
                        "marks": []
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "p,q",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " are primes.",
                        "marks": []
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "p10-q33-e-short-reason",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "fontSize": null,
                    "align": "left",
                    "spaceBefore": 0,
                    "spaceAfter": 2,
                    "lineHeight": 1.4,
                    "indent": 0,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "For odd ",
                        "marks": []
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "n>5",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": ", ",
                        "marks": []
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "n-3",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " is even and greater than 2, so the assumed conjecture applies.",
                        "marks": []
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              "worked": "Let $n>5$ be odd. Then $n-3$ is even and greater than 2. By the assumed Goldbach conjecture, there are primes $p$ and $q$ such that\n$$n-3=p+q.$$\nTherefore,\n$$n=3+p+q.$$\nSince 3 is prime, $n$ is the sum of three primes.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 52
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
